{"title": "Foundations of Information Retrieval", "learningObjectives": [
    "Define and explain the core components of a search engine architecture, including indexing, retrieval, and ranking.",
    "Compare traditional statistical retrieval models (TF-IDF, BM25) with modern neural retrieval techniques and their respective strengths.",
    "Apply standard evaluation metrics to assess search engine performance and identify common failure modes in retrieval systems."
  ], "beats": [
    {
      "title": "Search Engine Architecture Overview",
      "claim": "Search engines organize documents into structured indexes that map queries to relevant records using defined data types and access paths.",
      "example": "A document database stores text in tables with columns for title, content, and metadata, using primary keys to uniquely identify each record.",
      "visualDirection": "Display a simplified block diagram showing a query input, an index lookup module, a retrieval stage, and a ranking output, with arrows indicating data flow.",
      "sourceIds": ["postgresql-documentation"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Query Processing and Indexing",
      "claim": "Indexes provide additional access paths for selected workloads, enabling fast document retrieval without scanning the entire corpus.",
      "example": "A term frequency index stores each unique word alongside its document IDs, allowing the system to instantly locate all documents containing a specific query term.",
      "visualDirection": "Show a side-by-side comparison of a full corpus scan versus an index lookup, highlighting the reduced number of records examined in the latter.",
      "sourceIds": ["postgresql-documentation"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Traditional Statistical Retrieval Models",
      "claim": "Traditional retrieval models like TF-IDF and BM25 calculate relevance scores by weighing term frequency against document length and term rarity across the collection.",
      "example": "In a TF-IDF calculation, the term 'machine' receives a high score in a short technical manual but a low score in a lengthy general encyclopedia article.",
      "visualDirection": "Render a bar chart comparing TF-IDF scores for a single query term across three documents of varying lengths and term frequencies.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-636"]
    },
    {
      "title": "BM25 Ranking Formula",
      "claim": "BM25 refines traditional statistical approaches by incorporating position weighting and a more robust estimation of document length to improve precision.",
      "example": "BM25 assigns higher relevance scores to documents where the query term appears near the beginning, reflecting stronger user intent alignment.",
      "visualDirection": "Illustrate a document snippet with highlighted query terms, showing position-weighted score increments for earlier occurrences versus later ones.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-636"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Modern Neural Retrieval Techniques",
      "claim": "Modern retrieval techniques employ neural reranking and retrieval models that learn complex semantic mappings between queries and documents through deep learning.",
      "example": "A neural reranker takes a list of initially retrieved documents and reorders them by predicting the probability that each document satisfies the user's underlying intent.",
      "visualDirection": "Display a two-stage pipeline graphic: a fast retrieval stage followed by a neural reranking stage that outputs a refined ranked list.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-636"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Search Engine Evaluation Metrics",
      "claim": "Search engine evaluation relies on standardized metrics that measure ranking quality, relevance, and user satisfaction across test queries.",
      "example": "Precision at K measures the proportion of relevant documents among the top K results, providing a direct measure of immediate query satisfaction.",
      "visualDirection": "Show a table comparing Precision@10 and Recall@10 scores for three different retrieval models on a benchmark dataset.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-636"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Search Applications and Conversational IR",
      "claim": "Search applications extend traditional retrieval into interactive domains like conversational IR and enterprise search, adapting to dynamic user contexts.",
      "example": "A conversational IR system maintains session state to resolve pronouns and follow-up questions, improving relevance across multiple turns.",
      "visualDirection": "Present a dialogue flow diagram showing a user query, system context tracking, and an improved retrieval result for a follow-up question.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-636"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Data Quality and Retrieval Integrity",
      "claim": "Data quality analysis makes missingness, duplicates, and inconsistent representation explicit, ensuring retrieval systems process reliable and clean inputs.",
      "example": "Before indexing, a preprocessing step removes duplicate document entries and standardizes formatting to prevent skewed ranking results.",
      "visualDirection": "Visualize a data cleaning workflow with icons representing deduplication, normalization, and missing-value handling before the indexing stage.",
      "sourceIds": ["postgresql-documentation"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Evaluation and Error Analysis",
      "claim": "Evaluation must match the task and the cost of errors, while distribution shift, missing data, and unexamined sampling choices can invalidate conclusions about system performance.",
      "example": "A retrieval model trained on technical documents may fail on general queries due to distribution shift, requiring separate validation sets for each domain.",
      "visualDirection": "Display a confusion matrix or error breakdown chart showing false positives and false negatives across different query types.",
      "sourceIds": ["scikit-learn-user-guide"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Practical Analysis Checklist",
      "claim": "Students must apply durable concepts and problem-solving methods to design and execute experiments for search engine components, ensuring reproducible and valid results.",
      "example": "A group project requires defining a baseline retrieval model, selecting a validation split, running cross-validation, and documenting how preprocessing choices affected final rankings.",
      "visualDirection": "List a step-by-step checklist graphic covering experiment design, data splitting, model training, evaluation, and documentation requirements.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-636", "scikit-learn-user-guide"]
    }
  ]}
